{
	title: 'Find: ranked, streamed search over all sessions',
	spec: `The host answers a find query with results streamed in tiers, cheapest first, each tier sent as soon as it is ready and a newer query cancelling the old one:
1. Session metadata: id (prefix "158-" matches every 158-* session), name, model, cwd. Instant, from memory.
2. User and assistant text, then thinking, then tool calls (input and description), then tool output and other records.
A tier is a block kind; within a kind, score = match quality (whole word > prefix > substring; case-insensitive; every word of the query must match) times recency (half-life about three days, so a one-day-old tool result can beat a ten-day-old user message). Results carry session id and name, block id, kind, age and a one-line snippet around the match, and link to that block (task 0z addresses). Filters restrict kinds (user/assistant, thinking, tools, other), and a query may say in:<kind>, model:<name>, cwd:<path>, since:<age>.
Index: a rebuildable projection in state/find.sqlite (bun:sqlite FTS5, porter-free unicode61 tokenizer plus a trigram table for substrings), never a source of truth: deleting it only costs a rebuild. The host updates it as history lines are appended and, at startup, catches up per session from its last indexed byte offset in the background (task 7j: chunks, never blocking the loop over 10 ms). Blob contents are indexed too, capped per block (first 64 kB). Until the index has caught up, the tier falls back to scanning histories newest first, so results arrive anyway. /find <query> prints the top results as a list with links; Ctrl-F opens the dialog (next task).

The projection and history scanning run in a dedicated Bun worker, with bounded reads and small transactions: not even one unusually large history record or SQLite merge may block the host event loop. Queries and cancellation are transient per-connection protocol messages, independent of following a session; result batches replace one tier at a time (top 50 each), and a terminal done/error event leaves those results intact. /find merges tiers by score and prints the top 20, using stable host-relative links that terminal Markdown expands with the current web login code. Filters accept in:text/user/assistant/thinking/tools/tool-call/tool-output/other/metadata, model and cwd case-insensitive substrings, since ages in minutes/hours/days/weeks (m/h/d/w). Blank queries show no results; filter-only queries are allowed.`,
	status: 'planned',
	notes: [
		'Host API for 6m: send {type: "find", request: <fresh string>, query: <string>, kinds?: ["text", "thinking", "tools", "other"]}; no sessionId or open session needed. Omitted kinds enables all; [] enables metadata only. A newer find on that connection cancels its previous query. {type: "find-cancel"} cancels on dialog close, as does disconnect. Standard command id/ack applies. Replies go only to the requesting connection: {type: "find-results", request, tier, results, done, scanning?, error?}. Ignore a request not current. Tier order: metadata, user, assistant, thinking, tool-call, tool-output, other. Each non-done batch REPLACES that tier with its current ranked top 50 (not a delta); scanning counts histories still being scanned for this tier. done:true is a terminal status, NOT a replacement (results is empty); error is a terminal failure. Result: {sessionId,name,blockId,kind,age,score,snippet,href}; age is milliseconds, metadata blockId is empty, href is /<session>#<stable block id> or /<session>. Snippets are plain one-line text, no markup; the dialog highlights query words locally. Tier buffers let 6m preserve selection and append lower tiers; numeric scores are comparable across tiers, and /find merges globally by score.',
		'Implementation architecture: host find.ts dispatches to one dedicated Bun Worker (find-worker.ts); find-index.ts owns bun:sqlite FTS5 unicode61 and trigram tables, byte-offset marks, bounded history read buffers and 16-block transactions. Parsing even a giant ASON record and expensive SQLite statements happen only on the worker, never on the host event loop. Worker scan/index loops yield after about 4ms and between transactions; this is independent of 7j host startup work. The metadata catalog is loaded in background into worker memory using sessions.load/liveFile; open-session metadata accompanies searches, append notifications coalesce on a zero-delay host timer. No disk/history writes in the search projection path except state/find.sqlite. Substring queries shorter than three codepoints scan indexed rows; FTS candidates are always verified by the common matcher (every query word, whole word 3 > prefix 2 > substring 1; sum times three-day recency decay).'
	],
	needs: ['gj', 'x0']
}
